    <computation xml:id="information-from-sign-of-constant-term">
      <title>Factoring a quadratic trinomial into a product of binomials</title>
      <p>
        Suppose we are given <m>Ax^2+Bx+C</m> with <m>A=1</m>, meaning we have <m>x^2+Bx+C</m>.
        To factor <m>x^2+Bx+C</m> as the product of binomials:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the costant term <m>C</m> is positive, then the signs contained in both binomials match.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the costant term <m>C</m> is negative, then the signs contained in both binomials differ.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>      
    </computation>
    

    <p>
      Now, let's dig in further to see what happens when the constant term is postive.
      First, let's note that when we have the consstant term positive, then the two binomials have to have the same sign, 
      meaning that both signs are positive or both signs are negative.
      But now, we can see two situations:
      the linear term can be positive or negative.
      For example, we can have <m>x^2+9x+20</m> or we can have <m>x^2-9x+20</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      It seems like a reasonable guess (and we'll check if it works) that if we have a <m>+9x</m> term,
      that the matching sign in both binomials is a plus, while if we have a <m>-9x</m> term,
      then the matching sign in both binomials is a minus.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let's dig in to see if we can find the missing values in <me>(x+\_\_\_)(x+\_\_\_) = x^2+9x+20.</me>
      To match the notation earlier, let's fill in these blanks with <m>b</m> and <m>d</m>.
      That is, let's find the <m>b</m> and <m>d</m> in
      <me>(x+b)(x+d)=x^2+9x+20</me>
      by expanding the left side:
      <me>x^2+dx+bx+bd = x^2+9x+20.</me>
      Now, the middle two terms <m>dx</m> and <m>bx</m> are like terms, and we can <q>collect like terms</q> here,
      though what we'll really do is apply <xref ref="collecting-like-terms-justification" /> and factor out <m>x</m> for the middle two terms:
      <me>x^2+(d+b)x+bd = x^2+9x+20.</me>
      The last term on the left <m>bd</m> has no <m>x</m>, so this must be what matches up with <m>20</m> on the right.
      The linear coefficient (the number in front of <m>x</m>) on the left is <m>d+b</m> and on the right is <m>9</m>.
      This combination of things tells us what we need. The two numbers we need to find (called <m>b</m> and <m>d</m> here)
      need to multiply to <m>20</m> and need to add to <m>9</m>.
      Those two numbers are <m>4</m> and <m>5</m> so we can factor <m>x^2+9x+20</m> as either
      <me>(x+4)(x+5)</me>
      or 
      <me>(x+5)(x+4).</me>
      These are essentially the same (just switching the order of the factors), and we can check our factoring by expanding.
      Earlier, we found the two numbers which multiply to <m>20</m> and add to <m>9</m>.
      Here is a systematic way that we can do that: list pairs of numbers whose product is <m>20</m>:
      <ul>
        <li><p><m>1</m> and <m>20</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>2</m> and <m>10</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>4</m> and <m>5</m></p></li>
      </ul>
      To be systematic, we started with the smallest whole number as a factor, and kept working our way up.
      Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>9</m>.
      In this case, it happens to be <m>4</m> and <m>5</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For <m>x^2-9x+20</m>, we'll need minus signs inside both binomials. That is, our set up looks like:
      <me>(x-\_\_\_)(x-\_\_\_) = x^2-9x+20.</me>
      Now, the minus signs are <emph>already</emph> drawn in, so we just need to fill in numbers <emph>after</emph> the minus signs.
      Based on the <m>20</m> and the number after the minus sign but before the <m>x</m> being <m>9</m>,
      we are again looking for two numbers whose product is <m>20</m> and whose sum is <m>9</m>, and these are again <m>4</m> and <m>5</m>,
      which fill in the two blanks:
      <me>(x-4)(x-5)</me>
      or alternately,
      <me>(x-5)(x-4).</me>
      It is always a little more confusing to describe, which is why we are using the following language: in
      <me>(x-\_\_\_)(x-\_\_\_) = x^2-9x+20</me>
      we have <emph>already</emph> written in the minus signs, so we just need to include write <emph>after</emph>
      the minus signs the two positive numbers that add to <m>9</m> and multiply to <m>20</m>.      
    </p>  

    <computation xml:id="factor-positive-constant">
      <title>Factoring a quadratic trinomial with a positive constant term</title>
      <p>
        To factor <m>x^2+Bx+C</m> with constant term <m>C &gt; 0</m>, the signs contained in both binomials are the same.
        Are both signs positive or both signs negative?
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the linear coefficient <m>B</m> is positive, then the signs contained in both binomials are plus signs.
            </p>
            <p>
              In this template
              <me>(x+\_\_\_)(x+\_\_\_)</me>
              we need to fill the blanks with positive numbers that multiply to <m>C</m> and add to <m>B</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the linear coefficient <m>B</m> is negative, then the signs contained in both binomials are minus signs.
            </p>
            <p>
              In this template
              <me>(x-\_\_\_)(x-\_\_\_)</me>
              the minus signs are already drawn in the factoring, 
              and we need to fill the blanks with positive numbers that multiply to <m>C</m> and add to <m>|B|</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>      
    </computation>

    <example xml:id="factor-x2-plus-11x-plus-24">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2+11x+24</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Because we have a quadratic trinomial with positive constant term <m>24</m> and we are being asked to factor,
          <xref ref="factor-positive-constant" /> applies.
          We are looking for two numbers to fill in the blanks in
          <me>(x+\_\_\_)(x+\_\_\_).</me>
          Examining the constant term <m>24</m> and the linear coefficient <m>11</m>,
          the two blanks must be filled with positive numbers that multiply to <m>24</m> and add to <m>11</m>.
          To do this, we can list pairs of numbers whose product is <m>24</m>:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>1</m> and <m>24</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>2</m> and <m>12</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>3</m> and <m>8</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>4</m> and <m>6</m></p></li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>11</m>.
          These are <m>3</m> and <m>8</m>, in either order, so we can factor <m>x^2+11x+24</m> as
          <me>(x+3)(x+8)</me>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>    

    <example xml:id="factor-x2-minus-12x-plus-35">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2-12x+35</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Because we have a quadratic trinomial with positive constant term <m>35</m> and we are being asked to factor,
          <xref ref="factor-positive-constant" /> applies. 
          We are looking for two numbers to fill in the blanks in
          <me>(x-\_\_\_)(x-\_\_\_).</me>
          Examining the constant term <m>35</m> and the linear coefficient <m>-12</m>,
          the two blanks must be filled with positive numbers that multiply to <m>35</m> and add to <m>12</m>.
          (We are looking at <m>12</m> because the absolute value of <m>-12</m> is <m>12</m>.)
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>35</m>:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>1</m> and <m>35</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>5</m> and <m>7</m></p></li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>12</m>.
          These are <m>5</m> and <m>7</m>, in either order, so we can factor <m>x^2-12x+35</m> as
          <me>(x-5)(x-7)</me>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2-8x+15</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Since the constant term <m>15</m> is positive, and the linear coefficient <m>-8</m> is negative, our set up for factoring is
          <me>(x-\_\_\_)(x-\_\_\_).</me>
          The blanks must be filled with positive numbers that multiply to <m>15</m> and add to <m>8</m>.
          (Note again, we are looking for positive numbers: the minus signs have already been drawn in, and those minus signs will be included in our final answer.
          The blanks themselves need to be filled with positive numbers.)
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>15</m>:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>1</m> and <m>15</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>3</m> and <m>5</m></p></li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>8</m>.
          These are <m>3</m> and <m>5</m> so our factorization is
          <me>(x-3)(x-5)</me>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      We can check our work. Starting with our factorization,
      <md>
        <mrow>(x-3)(x-5) \amp= x^2- 5 \cdot x - 3 \cdot x + 15</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= x^2 - 8x + 15</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2+14x+40</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Because we have a quadratic trinomial with positive constant term <m>40</m> and we are being asked to factor,
          <xref ref="factor-positive-constant" /> applies.
          We are looking for two numbers to fill in the blanks in
          <me>(x+\_\_\_)(x+\_\_\_).</me>
          Examining the constant term <m>40</m> and the linear coefficient <m>14</m>,
          the two blanks must be filled with positive numbers that multiply to <m>40</m> and add to <m>14</m>.
          We list the pairs of numbers whose product is <m>40</m>:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>1</m> and <m>40</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>2</m> and <m>20</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>4</m> and <m>10</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>5</m> and <m>8</m></p></li>
          </ul>
          Then, look through the list to see which pair of numbers adds up to <m>14</m>.
          These are <m>4</m> and <m>10</m>, in either order, so we can factor <m>x^2+14x+40</m> as
          <me>(x+4)(x+10)</me>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>
    <p>
      We can check our work. Starting with our factorization,
      <md>
        <mrow>(x+4)(x+10) \amp= x^2 + 10 \cdot x + 4 \cdot x + 40</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp= x^2 + 14x + 40</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>


        <p>
      The recipe we gave in <xref ref="factor-positive-constant" /> is very handy for factoring, but only works when the constant term is positive.
      Let's do a short exploration to develop a strategy for factoring when the constant term is negative.
      In the strategy that we're about to develop, we will be creating a template in which minus signs and plus signs are already in place.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            When we expand <m>(x-7)(x+2)</m> we get <m>x^2+2x-7x-14 = x^2 - 5x - 14</m>.
            In the factored version, the numbers after the minus sign and the plus sign are <m>7</m> and <m>2</m>.
            (Note, by saying the number <emph>after</emph> the minus sign, we mean to intentionally not include the minus sign itself.)
            The difference between <m>7</m> and <m>2</m> is <m>5</m>, which is the number after the first minus sign in <m>x^2-5x-14</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            When we expand <m>(x+7)(x-2)</m> we get <m>x^2-2x+7x-14 = x^2 + 5x - 14</m>.
            In the factored version, the numbers after the minus sign and plus sign are <m>7</m> and <m>2</m> again, just as they were i nthe previous exploration.
            The difference between <m>7</m> and <m>2</m> is <m>5</m>, which is the number after the first plus sign in <m>x^2+5x-14</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      In both <m>x^2-5x-14</m> and <m>x^2+5x-14</m>, the minus in front of <m>14</m> means that the signs in the middle of the factors
      for the factorization of <emph>both</emph> of these quadratics must include one minus sign and one plus sign.
      In both factorizations, we saw the numbers <m>7</m> and <m>2</m> appear, and their difference is <m>5</m>,
      which appears to be connected to the linear coefficient of either <m>5</m> or <m>-5</m>, depending on which quadratic expression we look at.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            In the case of <m>x^2-5x-14</m>, this negative linear coefficient <m>-5</m> in front of <m>x</m> came from collecting the like terms <m>2x-7x</m> in <m>x^2+2x-7x-14</m>, 
            and for the <m>2x-7x</m> to occur, the factorization required the <m>7</m> to appear after the minus sign and the <m>2</m> to appear after the plus sign.
            (To say this in the form of a shortcut, because the linear coefficient was negative, we needed more <q>negative <m>x</m></q> than <q>positive <m>x</m></q>.)            
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            In the case of <m>x^2+5x-14</m>, the positive linear coefficient <m>5</m> in front of <m>x</m> came from collecting like terms <m>-2x+7x</m>
            in <m>x^2-2x+7x-14</m>, which can only occur if the factorization has the <m>2</m> appearing after the minus sign and the <m>7</m> appearing before the minus sign.
            In short, to get the linear coefficient to be positive, we needed more <emph>positive</emph> than <emph>negative</emph> when we collect like terms.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      This creates for us an overall strategy to factor a quadratic when the quadratic coefficient is <m>1</m> (often a hidden <m>1</m>) and the constant term is negative.
    </p>

    <computation xml:id="factor-negative-constant">
      <title>Factoring a quadratic trinomial with a negative constant term</title>
      <p>
        To factor <m>x^2+Bx-C</m> or <m>x^2-Bx-C</m> with a negative constant term,
        the signs contained in both binomials will be different.
        In other words, one set of parentheses will have a plus sign, and the other will have a minus sign.
        Find two numbers whose product is <m>C</m> and whose difference is <m>B</m>.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the linear coefficient is positive, place the bigger number after the plus sign and the smaller number after the minus sign.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the linear coefficient is negative, place the bigger number after the minus sign and the smaller number after the plus sign.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>      
    </computation>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 + 5x - 14</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We need to find two numbers that whose product is <m>14</m> and whose difference is <m>5</m>.
          These numbers are <m>7</m> and <m>2</m>.
          Because the linear coefficient <m>5</m> is positive, we place the bigger number <m>7</m> after the plus sign
          and the smaller number <m>2</m> after the minus sign.
          <me>x^2 + 5x - 14 = (x+7)(x-2)</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

        <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 10x - 24</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We need to find two numbers that whose product is <m>24</m> and whose difference is <m>10</m>.
          These numbers are <m>12</m> and <m>2</m>.
          Because the linear coefficient <m>-10</m> is negative, we place the bigger number <m>12</m> after the minus sign
          and the smaller number <m>2</m> after the plus sign.
          <me>x^2 - 10x - 24 = (x-12)(x+2)</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

        <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each quadratic below, notice the quadratic coefficient is <m>1</m>.
          Based on the linear and constant terms, identify what we need to look for to factor each expression.
          (If you wish to practice by factoring, feel free to do so! 
          However, this question is intentionally worded to get us practicing how to ask the right questions!)
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>x^2 + 6x - 16</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x^2 + 14x + 33</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x^2 + 16x + 60</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x^2 + 4x - 60</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>x^2 - 8x - 20</m></p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              To factor <m>x^2 + 6x - 16</m>, find two numbers whose product is <m>16</m> and whose difference is <m>6</m>.
              The negative constant term <m>-16</m> told us we need a difference.
              The negative constant term also tells us the factorization has one sign positive and one sign negative.
              Because the linear coefficient is positive (namely <m>6</m>), of the two numbers we find, the bigger number goes after the plus sign.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              To factor <m>x^2 + 14x + 33</m>, find two numbers whose product is <m>33</m> and whose sum is <m>14</m>.
              The positive constant term <m>33</m> told us we need a sum.
              The positive constant term also tells us the factorization has the same sign twice, 
              and the positive linear coefficient (namely <m>14</m>) tells us both signs positive.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              To factor <m>x^2 + 16x + 60</m>, find two numbers whose product is <m>60</m> and whose sum is <m>16</m>.
              The positive constant term <m>60</m> told us we need a sum.
              The positive constant term also tells us the factorization has the same sign twice, 
              and the positive linear coefficient (namely <m>16</m>) tells us both signs positive.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              To factor <m>x^2 + 4x - 60</m>, find two numbers whose product is <m>60</m> and whose difference is <m>4</m>.
              The negative constant term <m>-60</m> told us we need a difference.
              The negative constant term also tells us the factorization has one sign positive and one sign negative.
              Because the linear coefficient is positive (namely <m>4</m>), of the two numbers we find, the bigger number goes after the plus sign.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              To factor <m>x^2 - 8x - 20</m>, find two numbers whose product is <m>20</m> and whose difference is <m>8</m>.
              The negative constant term <m>-20</m> told us we need a difference.
              The negative constant term also tells us the factorization has one sign positive and one sign negative.
              Because the linear coefficient is negative (namely <m>-8</m>), of the two numbers we find, the bigger number goes after the minus sign.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </answer>
    </example>


    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 49</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can rewrite the given expression as <m>x^2 + 0x - 49</m>.
          Based on <xref ref="factor-negative-constant" />, we are looking for two numbers whose product is <m>49</m> and whose difference is <m>0</m>.
          The fact that the difference is zero means that we need the same number twice (since a number minus itself is zero).
          Those numbers are <m>7</m> and <m>7</m>, and we place one of these behind a minus sign and the other behind a plus sign:
          <me>x^2-49 = (x+7)(x-7)</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 100</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can rewrite the given expression as <m>x^2 + 0x - 100</m>.
          Based on <xref ref="factor-negative-constant" />, we are looking for two numbers whose product is <m>100</m> and whose difference is <m>0</m>.
          Those numbers are <m>10</m> and <m>10</m>, and we place one of these behind a minus sign and the other behind a plus sign:
          <me>x^2 - 100 = (x+10)(x-10)</me>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

        <p>
      When factoring quadratics where the quadratic coefficient is <m>1</m>, we are looking for two numbers to create a certain product,
      and in addition, the two numbers need to create a certain sum or a certain difference.
      (We are looking for a sum when the constant term is positive, and a difference when the constant term is negative.)
    </p>


        <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 + 6x + 9</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We can apply earlier principles to find two numbers whose product is <m>9</m> and whose sum is <m>6</m>.
          These numbers are <m>3</m> and <m>3</m>, so 
          <me>x^2 + 6x + 9 = (x+3)(x+3)</me>
          and we can optionally rewrite this as <m>(x+3)^2</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>


        <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 12x + 36</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking for two numbers whose product is <m>36</m> and whose sum is <m>12</m>.
          These numbers are <m>6</m> and <m>6</m>. The factorization is
          <me>(x-6)(x-6)</me>
          and we can optionally rewrite this as <m>(x-6)^2</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>


        <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Factor <m>x^2 - 8x + 16</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          We are looking for two numbers whose product is <m>16</m> and whose sum is <m>8</m>.
          These numbers are <m>4</m> and <m>4</m>. The factorization is
          <me>(x-4)(x-4)</me>
          and we can optionally rewrite this as <m>(x-4)^2</m>.
        </p>
      </answer>
    </example>

          <p>
            To factor quadratics, in the case of a quadratic coefficient of <m>A=1</m>, 
            there is a shortcut method that establishes the signs first and allows us to then only think about placing positive numbers, with the signs already in place.
          </p>
